# Real finding, confirmed this session: mkosi runs `systemctl preset-all`
# itself as one of its own final build steps -- a plain `systemctl
# enable` in a postinst script gets silently undone by that (confirmed:
# "Removed '/buildroot/etc/.../slfhst-install.service'" in a real build
# log). A preset file is what actually survives, same lesson already
# learned for the appliance build's own units -- consistent mechanism,
# not a special case.
enable slfhst-install.service

# Real finding from actually boot-testing the ISO under QEMU: base
# systemd's own systemd-firstboot.service runs its interactive
# "Initial Setup" wizard (timezone/root password prompts) on tty1
# before slfhst-install.service gets a chance to -- this project's own
# installer already handles all of that through its own flow, so
# Fedora's generic one is pure noise here, not a fallback worth keeping.
# Lower-numbered preset files win ties, so this line (in 80-*) overrides
# whatever base systemd's own 90-systemd.preset says for this unit.
disable systemd-firstboot.service
